<h2>Can Dogs Eat Pineapple?</h2>
<p>Yes, and this is one of the most-searched food questions dog owners ask. A few small chunks of fresh pineapple, with the tough core and spiky skin removed, is a fine occasional treat. It is high in natural sugar, though, so a dog with diabetes or weight issues should skip it, and more than a small amount can cause loose stool simply because of the fiber and sugar load, not because pineapple is toxic.</p>

<h2>Is Peanut Butter Good for Dogs?</h2>
<p>Generally yes, and it is one of the most common ways owners hide pills or stuff a puzzle toy. The one thing that actually matters here is the label: xylitol, a sugar substitute, is highly toxic to dogs even in small amounts, and more peanut butter brands are quietly adding it than you would expect. Check the ingredient list every single time, not just the first time you buy a jar, since brands reformulate.</p>

<h2>Can Dogs Eat Raw Eggs?</h2>
<p>This one is more debated than most. Raw eggs carry a salmonella risk, similar to raw eggs for people, and raw egg whites specifically contain avidin, which can interfere with biotin absorption if fed regularly over time. A fully cooked egg, scrambled or boiled with nothing added, avoids both issues and still gives your dog the protein. If you are set on raw, talk to your vet about how your dog's diet is balanced overall.</p>

<h2>Is Olive Oil Good for Dogs?</h2>
<p>In small amounts, yes. A teaspoon drizzled over food now and then is not going to cause harm, and some owners use it for coat shine. It is still fat, though, and too much of it, especially all at once, can trigger loose stool or, in a dog prone to it, contribute to pancreatitis. Moderation is the actual guidance here, not a flat yes or no.</p>

<h2>What About Pumpkin Seeds, Tofu, and Sweet Potato?</h2>
<p>All three are generally fine in small, plain amounts. Pumpkin seeds should be unsalted and ideally ground or ground slightly so they are easier to digest. Tofu is not toxic but also is not doing much nutritionally for a dog, so treat it as an occasional novelty rather than a diet staple. Sweet potato, cooked and unseasoned, is actually a common ingredient in commercial dog foods already, so a few plain bites are a non-issue.</p>

<h2>The Foods That Are Genuinely Not Worth the Risk</h2>
<p>A few items come up often enough to flag specifically. Corn on the cob is not about the corn itself, which is fine; the cob is the problem, since it does not break down and can lodge in the intestines, sometimes requiring surgery to remove. Shrimp tails, shells and the fatty trimmings off meat carry a similar physical risk, plus a high-fat load that can trigger pancreatitis in a sensitive dog. Sauerkraut is often overlooked because it sounds like a vegetable, but most jarred versions are loaded with sodium and some recipes include onion or garlic, both of which are genuinely toxic to dogs in any form, cooked or raw.</p>

<h2>If Your Dog Already Ate Something Questionable</h2>
<p>If your dog got into chocolate, grapes, onions, xylitol-containing food, or alcohol, do not wait to see what happens. Call the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center at (888) 426-4435 or Pet Poison Helpline at (855) 764-7661 (24 hours; a fee may apply), or your own vet. For a food on the "caution" list, like raw egg or a large amount of olive oil, watch for vomiting, diarrhea or lethargy over the next day and call your vet if anything shows up. Our <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a> and <a href="/tools/dog-toxic-foods-guide/">complete list of foods toxic to dogs</a> cover the full picture beyond what is in this article.</p>

<h2>A Few Common Questions</h2>

<h3>Can dogs eat rice?</h3>
<p>Yes. Plain, cooked white rice is easy to digest and is actually a common ingredient in bland diets vets recommend for dogs recovering from an upset stomach.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat chia seeds?</h3>
<p>In small amounts, yes. They are not toxic, but they absorb liquid and swell, so feed them already soaked rather than dry, and keep the portion small.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat ground turkey?</h3>
<p>Plain, cooked, unseasoned ground turkey is fine and is used in some commercial and home-cooked dog diets. Skip turkey seasoned with garlic, onion powder or heavy salt.</p>

<h3>Is chicken good for dogs?</h3>
<p>Yes, plain cooked chicken without the bones, skin or seasoning is one of the more common proteins in dog food for a reason. Bones, cooked especially, splinter and are a genuine choking and perforation risk.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat parsley?</h3>
<p>A small amount of curly parsley is fine and is sometimes used as a breath freshener. Avoid large amounts of flat-leaf (Italian) parsley specifically, which in large quantities has been linked to photosensitivity in some animals.</p>

<h3>Is peppermint oil safe for dogs?</h3>
<p>Not as an essential oil applied to the skin or given orally; concentrated essential oils, including peppermint, eucalyptus and others, can be toxic to dogs. A tiny amount of mint as an herb is different from the concentrated oil, which should be kept away from pets entirely.</p>

<h3>Can dogs eat potato skins?</h3>
<p>Cooked, plain potato skin is not toxic, but raw potato and green-tinted potato skin contain solanine, which is genuinely harmful. Stick to fully cooked, unseasoned potato if you share any at all.</p>

